        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:38:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128196,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Congenital smooth muscle hamartoma; acquired smooth muscle hamartoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:38:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128197,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The majority of cases occur on the trunk or extremities; the head and neck region and external genitalia are less common sites of involvement {32804407}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:38:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128198,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is a slightly indurated and pigmented or flesh-coloured plaque, ranging from millimetres to several centimetres in size, with or without perifollicular papules and hypertrichosis. A positive pseudo&ndash;Darier sign is present in 80% of congenital SMHs (CSMHs). Most CSMHs are solitary; however, rare multiple, familial, and non-familial cases have been documented {22630577}. Diffuse CSMH has been described as a component of Michelin tyre baby syndrome {8436660}, and cases of combined SMH with congenital or blue naevi have also been reported {18544054; 8436660}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:38:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128199,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>SMH has a slight male predominance. CSMH is uncommon, affecting approximately 1&nbsp;in 2600&nbsp;births {2356798}. Acquired SMH is extremely rare, with &lt;&nbsp;20&nbsp;cases reported to date. Age at presentation ranges from 12&nbsp;to 64&nbsp;years {29774573; 34637157}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:38:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128200,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology is unknown. Abnormal migration and proliferation of arrector pili muscle during fetal development may play a role.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:38:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128201,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A subset of CSMHs result from postzygotic <em>ACTB</em> mutations. These mutations have been previously reported in Becker naevi, suggesting that CSMHs and Becker naevi are biologically related {32170967}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:38:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128202,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:38:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128203,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>SMH demonstrates haphazardly arranged, mature smooth muscle bundles in the dermis, often in close proximity to the pilosebaceous units {2658846}. This is in contrast to pilosebaceous leiomyoma, which demonstrates more orderly smooth muscle bundles arranged in ramifying fascicles. Atypia/pleomorphism and mitoses are absent. Focal retraction artefact may be observed around the smooth muscle bundles. Expression of smooth muscle markers is seen. Overlying epidermal hyperkeratosis, acanthosis, and basal hyperpigmentation, reminiscent of Becker naevus, may be encountered.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:38:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
